# XSH setjmp()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# setjmp() appears only in the contexts the page allows. Locals changed
# after setjmp() are volatile where their values are read after the jump.

assertion  XSH.setjmp.001
anchor     functions/setjmp.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/setjmp.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       A direct call of setjmp() returns 0 and saves the environment;
           longjmp() with the saved buffer then makes setjmp() return a
           non-zero value, once for each longjmp().
test       t/001-direct-and-jump.c

assertion  XSH.setjmp.002
anchor     functions/setjmp.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       setjmp() works in each context the page allows: as the whole
           controlling expression of if, switch and while; compared with an
           integral constant (== 0, != 0); under a unary !; and as an
           expression statement, possibly cast to void. In each, the direct
           return takes the 0 path and a longjmp() takes the other.
test       t/002-contexts.c

assertion  XSH.setjmp.003
anchor     functions/setjmp.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/setjmp.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       Calling setjmp() again with the same jmp_buf replaces the saved
           environment: a later longjmp() resumes from the second call, not
           the first.
test       t/003-replace.c
